0.6 w 54.00 747.00 m 558.00 747.00 l S
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 716.00 Tm (Body: Four field-tested adjustments the practitioners applied once the basic operator install was running.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 700.77 Tm (Items: Match vLLM's GPU memory utilization to the Ray placement fraction, because a 0.25 GPU request) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 685.55 Tm (on its own can still saturate 100 percent of the device and let PyTorch and vLLM each grab the whole VRAM) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 670.32 Tm ([The Code Architect - TwICQ_zP8Ys @ 00:07:14].) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 655.10 Tm (Do not trust nvidia-smi on time-sliced cards: on a 24 GB L4 with 5 replicas it showed 13 GB while the pod's) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 639.87 Tm (real usage was 4 GB [CNCF - Vw7DTM1SV60 @ 00:04:56].) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 624.65 Tm (Inside each tenant vCluster, reinstall the GPU operator with the toolkit and driver disabled, reapply and) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 609.42 Tm (re-patch time slicing, and refresh the node if the 4 replicas do not show [vCluster - KzJvhbdgvl8 @) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 594.20 Tm (00:02:09].) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 578.97 Tm (Set NVIDIA_DRIVER_CAPABILITIES to compute and utility when the model does not use video, keep the) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 563.75 Tm (nvidia.com/gpu limit explicit, and add a toleration keyed nvidia.com/gpu to clear the GPU node's) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 548.52 Tm (NoSchedule taint [DevOps & AI Toolkit - zuRKdveFuZ4 @ 00:07:02].) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 526.30 Tm (Section 3: Where Practitioners Disagree & Alternative Playbooks) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 504.07 Tm (The first split is the classic one, time slicing versus MIG, and the practitioners do not land in the same place.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 488.85 Tm (Emmanuel Corels Academy ran the same jobs shared and then on separate MIG instances on an A100,) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 473.62 Tm (with a distinct CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES per job, and at 50 users throughput moved from 0.76 to 1.0) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 458.40 Tm (requests per second per GPU, yet mean speech latency came in lower with time sharing [sRtvITK_o8I @) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 443.17 Tm (00:03:35]. So the answer depends on whether a tenant is buying throughput or responsiveness. Julien) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 427.95 Tm (Semaan of Kubex concedes in Q&A that MIG is usually faster but static, and his entire build targets making) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 412.72 Tm (time slicing memory-safe rather than abandoning it [Vw7DTM1SV60 @ 00:20:40]. Kevin Klues frames it as a) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 397.50 Tm (fundamental inherent trade-off: MIG buys full fault isolation and memory-bandwidth QoS but locks you into) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 382.27 Tm (fixed partition sizes, while time partitioning gives flexibility that you pay for in contention [nOgxv_R13Dg @) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 367.05 Tm (00:05:00]. The Code Architect settles the fight by workload class instead of ideology: time slicing for bursty) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 351.82 Tm (heterogeneous workloads, MPS for trusted and predictable ones, and MIG only when you need absolute) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 336.60 Tm (isolation [TwICQ_zP8Ys @ 00:08:03]. The operator read is that no single sharing mode wins every tenant) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 321.37 Tm (mix, so each tenant gets placed and priced by the load profile it actually runs, not by whichever mode the) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 306.15 Tm (platform team installed first.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 290.92 Tm (Fractional GPUs split the field a second time, over whether a fraction is a promise or an enforced wall.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 275.70 Tm (Saiyam and Mike Peterson at vCluster demo the KAI approach: annotate the pod with gpu-fraction 0.5 while) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 260.47 Tm (keeping the limit at 1 [sXpJ4vSc-cM @ 00:38:38]. Semaan warns that this controls placement only; KAI does) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 245.25 Tm (not enforce the fraction at runtime, so noisy neighbors remain possible, and his fix layers HAMi-core) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 230.02 Tm (underneath, intercepting CUDA calls through LD_PRELOAD so a 0.35 fraction becomes an 8,061 MB) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 214.80 Tm (CUDA_DEVICE_MEMORY_LIMIT [Vw7DTM1SV60 @ 00:10:30]. The Code Architect hits the same wall) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 199.57 Tm (from the Ray side: a 0.25 GPU request can still saturate the whole device because Ray only sets) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 184.35 Tm (CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES, so the fix lives in the application, tuning vLLM's GPU memory utilization to) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 169.12 Tm (match the Ray placement fraction [TwICQ_zP8Ys @ 00:06:22]. Yuan Chen's benchmarks push a third) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 153.90 Tm (playbook, MPS with explicit limits: capping a low-priority job at 10% gave the other job near-dedicated) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 138.67 Tm (performance under heavy load, where plain time slicing pinned the GPU at 100% and degraded badly) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 123.45 Tm ([nOgxv_R13Dg @ 00:20:45]. Semaan's own talk counters that MPS adds a daemon that becomes another) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 108.22 Tm (point of failure. The real choice is where enforcement lives: in the CUDA interception layer, in each) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 93.00 Tm (application's memory config, or in an extra daemon on every GPU node.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 77.77 Tm (The tenancy wrapper is the third fault line. vCluster's Saiyam and Mike Peterson argue namespaces provide) Tj ET
